{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015j4qXWAJgChQK97QfnddGj","type":"tool_result","content":"7:    <title>The Great Gatsby - Audiobook Prep Report</title>\n574:                <h1>The Great Gatsby</h1>\n611:                    <div class=\"label\">Main Characters</div>\n631:                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Mrs. Sigourney Howard, a reflective and observant woman, recounts her experiences in the summer of 1922, set against the glittering yet hollow backdrop of Long Island’s East and West Egg. She begins her story with her arrival in West Egg, where she rents a modest home and quickly becomes entangled in the lives of her cousin Daisy Buchanan and her domineering husband Tom, a man whose racist rhetoric and emotional cruelty unsettle her. Her introduction to the enigmatic Jay Gatsby occurs on a fateful night when she sees him standing on the lawn, arms outstretched toward a distant green light across the bay—a symbol of his unattainable dream. This moment marks the beginning of her deepening involvement with Gatsby, whose lavish parties and mysterious past intrigue her, even as she remains wary of the illusions he cultivates.\n633:As the summer unfolds, Mrs. Howard becomes increasingly drawn into the tangled web of relationships that define the era’s elite. She witnesses Tom’s affair with Myrtle Wilson, a vibrant but desperate woman whose life is crushed by the very system she seeks to escape. The revelation of Gatsby’s past—his real name, James Gatz, and his rise from poverty through a series of calculated reinventions—unfolds gradually, revealing his obsessive love for Daisy, whom he has idealized since their brief romance years before. Gatsby’s dream of rekindling their relationship is tested when Daisy, under pressure from Tom, confesses she never truly loved him, shattering Gatsby’s illusion. The emotional climax occurs at the Plaza Hotel, where Gatsby’s dream is publicly dismantled, and the fatal accident involving Myrtle Wilson follows, with Daisy driving the car that kills her.\n635:In the aftermath, Mrs. Howard grapples with the moral decay and emotional emptiness of the world she once admired. Gatsby, believing he must protect Daisy, takes the blame for the accident, only to be murdered by George Wilson, who has been manipulated into believing Gatsby was responsible. The funeral, attended by only a few, underscores Gatsby’s profound isolation despite his wealth. Mrs. Howard reflects on the futility of chasing an idealized past, recognizing that Gatsby’s dream was not about Daisy herself, but about a version of himself that could never exist. In her final moments, she walks through the overgrown grounds of Gatsby’s mansion, erasing a vulgar inscription and contemplating the tragic inevitability of the American Dream’s collapse—where ambition, identity, and love are ultimately consumed by the relentless current of time and societal indifference.</div>\n798:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental in West Egg, feeling a newfound sense of belonging after helping a stranger find the village; he observes the opulent mansions of West Egg and East Egg, then drives across the bay to dine with his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom at their grand Georgian Colonial mansion in East Egg, where he meets Jordan Baker and is struck by Tom’s domineering presence and Daisy’s fragile charm. During a tense evening on the rosy-coloured porch at dusk, with four candles flickering despite Daisy’s objection, Tom passionately defends white supremacy using The Rise of the Coloured Empires, while Daisy mocks his pretensions, shares a whimsical secret about the butler’s nose, and later confides in Nick with startling vulnerability, declaring she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool'; after Tom receives a mysterious phone call and returns, Daisy feigns enchantment with a nightingale to deflect attention, and the evening ends with Tom and Jordan retreating to the library while Nick and Daisy sit in silent, uneasy communion. Later, as Nick departs by car, Daisy calls out to him to wait, and upon stopping in his yard at West Egg, he sees a mysterious figure—later identified as Gatsby—standing alone on the lawn, arms outstretched toward the dark water, trembling as he gazes at a single green light at the end of a distant dock, before vanishing into the night.</div>\n812:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n827:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a summer Sunday afternoon, Tom Buchanan forcibly removes the narrator from a train stopped in the desolate Valley of Ashes, where they meet Myrtle Wilson at her husband George B. Wilson’s run-down garage, which houses only a dusty Ford wreck; Tom and Myrtle arrange to meet in New York, with Myrtle taking a separate train car to avoid detection, and she purchases an Airedale dog for $10 from a man resembling John D. Rockefeller. Arriving at Myrtle’s top-floor apartment at 158th Street, they find it cluttered with oversized tapestried furniture and a blurred photograph mistakenly identified as a hen but later revealed to be Myrtle’s mother; Tom opens whisky from a locked bureau, and Myrtle calls her sister Catherine and the McKees, who arrive as she changes into a cream-coloured chiffon dress, transforming her demeanor into exaggerated hauteur. The party descends into chaos when Myrtle, emboldened by alcohol and the pretense of sophistication, repeatedly shouts Daisy’s name, prompting Tom to violently break her nose, after which the narrator, exhausted and disturbed, leaves the apartment and spends the night sleeping in the cold waiting room of Pennsylvania Station.</div>\n837:                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n839:                    <span class=\"tag\">George B. Wilson</span>\n858:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator arriving by car at Gatsby’s mansion in West Egg on a Saturday evening, invited formally by a handwritten note delivered by a chauffeur in a robin’s-egg-blue uniform, only to find himself among a vast, chaotic crowd of uninvited guests arriving from New York by automobile. The garden erupts in revelry with a full orchestra, lavish buffets, dancing under colored lights, and rampant rumors about Gatsby’s past—including claims he killed a man or was a German spy—while the narrator wanders through the excess, meeting Jordan Baker and encountering eccentric guests such as the owl-eyed man who marvels at the authenticity of Gatsby’s uncut books. As the party peaks at midnight with opera and champagne, Gatsby remains unseen until the narrator, after drinking champagne, unexpectedly converses with him, learning of his recent hydroplane purchase and an invitation to fly along the Sound the next morning; amid growing drunken chaos—marital fights, a woman weeping black tears from mascara, and a car crash outside where a coupé loses a wheel and two bewildered men claim innocence—Gatsby stands apart, sober and formal, and later summons Jordan Baker alone, leaving her visibly shaken. The chapter closes with the narrator reflecting on his daily life in New York: commuting on foot to the Probity Trust, observing lonely young clerks at twilight, romanticizing strangers on Fifth Avenue, and rekindling a complex relationship with Jordan, whose dishonesty—evident in her lies about a borrowed car and a past golf scandal—fails to provoke moral outrage, even as he remains entangled in letters to a woman back home, haunted by memories of her perspiration on her lip during tennis.</div>\n864:                    <span class=\"tag\">Jay Gatsby</span>\n893:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a Sunday morning as church bells ring along the shore, Gatsby’s mansion becomes the center of a chaotic, glittering social scene filled with guests from East Egg, West Egg, and New York. The narrator recalls a detailed list of attendees—many of whom are wealthy, eccentric, or morally dubious—arriving by automobile, including the Chester Beckers, the Blackbucks, the Snells, the Dancies, and numerous figures connected to the film industry and organized crime. Guests mingle carelessly, spreading wild rumors about Gatsby’s past, such as his alleged ties to Von Hindenburg and the devil, while indulging in cocktails and flowers. Some, like Klipspringer, become regulars known as 'the boarder,' while others, like Ripley Snell, are already on the brink of ruin, having been run over by a car on Gatsby’s gravel drive. The atmosphere is one of superficial glamour masking moral decay and profound ignorance of the host.</div>\n928:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a July morning, Gatsby arrives at the narrator’s door in his luxurious cream-colored car with a three-note horn, inviting him to lunch and launching an elaborate, inconsistent account of his past—including Oxford education, European aristocratic life, and wartime heroism in Montenegro, backed by a medal and photograph. During the drive through West Egg, the valley of ashes, and across the Queensboro Bridge into New York City, Gatsby uses a white card to evade a traffic stop, hinting at hidden influence. At noon in a cellar on Forty-second Street, the narrator meets Meyer Wolfshiem, a grotesque figure with human molar cuff buttons, who recounts the 1919 murder of Rosy Rosenthal and reveals Gatsby fixed the 1919 World Series, shocking the narrator. Later, Jordan Baker reveals to Nick that Gatsby bought his mansion solely to be near Daisy, and arranges a tea meeting; on a rainy day, Gatsby sends a man to cut Nick’s grass and delivers a greenhouse of flowers. When Daisy arrives in a large open car, Gatsby is pale and trembling, nearly knocking over a clock, and their reunion is initially stiff and despairing until Nick leaves them alone, after which Gatsby shows Daisy his mansion, his collection of expensive shirts that move her to tears, and his opulent rooms including a gold toilet set and Merton College Library, restoring his belief in his dream. As the storm clears, Gatsby points to the green light at Daisy’s dock, now diminished in meaning, and presents clippings about her and a photograph of his mentor Dan Cody, while Klipspringer plays a poorly executed piano piece; Gatsby’s illusion of Daisy begins to crack as he becomes utterly absorbed in her voice, forgetting the narrator entirely.</div>\n934:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n963:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with a New York reporter arriving at Gatsby’s West Egg mansion seeking a statement, drawn by rumors that Gatsby lives in a secret boat disguised as a house, reflecting how his notoriety has spiraled into urban legend; the narrative then shifts to Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz, a poor North Dakota farm boy who, at seventeen, rowed out to Dan Cody’s yacht anchored off Lake Superior, adopted the name Jay Gatsby, and spent five years aboard the Tuolomee serving in roles from steward to jailor, profoundly shaped by Cody’s wealth and lifestyle—only to be cheated out of a $25,000 inheritance by Ella Kaye after Cody’s death, leaving him with nothing but the refined persona he had cultivated. Weeks later, the narrator visits Gatsby’s mansion on a Sunday afternoon when Tom Buchanan, Mr. Sloane, and a woman in a brown riding-habit arrive on horseback; Gatsby nervously offers them drinks and invites them to supper, but they subtly rebuff his hospitality and refuse to let him join their ride back. Later, Tom and Daisy attend one of Gatsby’s parties, where Daisy is both fascinated and repulsed by the chaotic, vulgar energy of West Egg, dances with Gatsby, but Tom grows increasingly suspicious and dismissive, ultimately declaring him a bootlegger; afterward, Gatsby confides in the narrator that Daisy didn’t enjoy herself and clings to the delusion that he can repeat the past, specifically that Daisy will tell Tom she never loved him so they can return to Louisville and restart their relationship as if five years had never passed, recalling the moment he first kissed her under the moonlight as the origin of his idealized vision.</div>\n969:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n998:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house, where Gatsby has dismissed his servants and replaced them with Wolfsheim’s relatives; during a tense lunch, Daisy kisses Gatsby and declares her love in front of Tom, who becomes alarmed and later reveals he has investigated Gatsby’s past, mocking his Oxford claim while Gatsby observes Daisy’s voice is 'full of money.' The group departs for New York in two cars—Gatsby driving his own yellow vehicle, Tom taking the blue coupé—and after a heated ride, they stop at Wilson’s garage in the Valley of Ashes, where Wilson, ill and desperate, reveals he plans to take Myrtle away, while Myrtle, mistaking Jordan for Tom’s wife, watches in jealous terror under the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg. At the Plaza Hotel, Tom confronts Gatsby with accusations of bootlegging via drugstores tied to Wolfsheim, and under pressure, Daisy admits she once loved Tom, shattering Gatsby’s idealized dream; Tom orders them to leave in Gatsby’s car, and they depart in silence. Later, Myrtle rushes out of the garage at dusk and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that flees, witnessed by Michaelis, who is unsure of its exact color; Tom and the narrator arrive at the scene to find Myrtle’s body covered on a worktable, Wilson hysterical, and Tom urgently denies involvement, claiming his blue coupé was not the car and later breaks down in tears, calling the driver a 'God damned coward.' That night, Nick waits outside the Buchanan house and finds Gatsby lurking in the shadows, who confesses Daisy was driving the fatal car but intends to take the blame and will wait outside her window all night; Nick sees through the pantry window that Tom and Daisy are quietly together in the kitchen, already reconciled, and Gatsby stands alone in the moonlight, vigilantly watching a house where his dream has already ended.</div>\n1006:                    <span class=\"tag\">Jay Gatsby</span>\n1014:                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n1016:                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n1031:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter unfolds across a single day and night, beginning with the narrator’s sleepless night haunted by a foghorn and his frantic rush to Gatsby’s mansion at dawn after hearing a taxi arrive, where he finds Gatsby dejected after seeing Daisy briefly appear at her window before turning off the light. Inside the vast, dusty, and neglected mansion, they search blindly for cigarettes and smoke on the open French windows of the drawing-room, during which Gatsby refuses to flee to Atlantic City or Montreal, insisting he must know Daisy’s fate, and reveals his past as a penniless officer who misled her into believing he was her social equal, binding himself to a dream she abandoned after their brief romance. Later, the narrator reflects on his own final afternoon with Daisy in a warm room filled with fire, where their silent intimacy and tender gestures—Daisy brushing her lips against his coat’s shoulder, he touching her fingers—reveal a love that eclipsed his ambitions, a moment deliberately crafted as a farewell before his departure. Meanwhile, George Wilson, shattered by Myrtle’s death, fixates on the yellow car and the dog-leash found in her bureau, declaring 'He murdered her' and later staring at the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg, muttering 'God sees everything' as he descends into religious delusion; after vanishing for hours, he is traced walking from his garage to Port Roosevelt, then to Gad’s Hill where he buys an uneaten sandwich and coffee, before arriving in West Egg by half-past two and asking for Gatsby’s house. At two o'clock, Gatsby dons his bathing suit, instructs the butler to bring calls to the pool, and carries the old pneumatic mattress alone to the pool, having stopped the chauffeur from using the damaged car; the butler waits until four a.m. for a call that never comes, signaling Gatsby’s final loss of hope. Gunshots are heard, and the narrator rushes to find Gatsby’s body floating in the pool, the mattress drifting with faint ripples, moments before the gardener discovers Wilson’s body in the grass, completing the tragedy. The narrator, later in his city office, attempts to call Gatsby four times only to find the line busy with a long-distance call from Detroit, and after a tense, abrupt phone call with Jordan Baker—who accuses him of being unfriendly and leaves for Southampton by car—he marks the 3:50 train and sits in silence, visibly unsettled.</div>\n1039:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n1045:                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n1066:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">In the aftermath of Gatsby’s death, his mansion is overrun by police, photographers, and reporters, while the coroner’s dismissal of Wilson as a 'madman' and Catherine’s false testimony simplify the narrative into a tale of grief-driven violence. The narrator, feeling sole responsibility for Gatsby’s isolation, desperately tries to reach Daisy and Meyer Wolfshiem—calling Information, sending a letter via butler, and waiting for a wire—but receives no response, only a disconnected long-distance call from Chicago that mistakenly believed Gatsby was alive. On the third day, Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives by train from Minnesota, visibly shaken and bundled in a cheap ulster, displaying a worn photograph of the mansion and a childhood book with a handwritten self-improvement schedule, revealing Gatsby’s early ambition. Only Gatz and a Lutheran minister attend the funeral in a drizzling rain, as Klipspringer calls to request his tennis shoes and Wolfshiem refuses to come, citing fear of being 'mixed up' in death. At the cemetery, the man with owl-eyed glasses, drenched and wiping rain from his spectacles, arrives unexpectedly, murmurs 'Amen to that' after hearing 'Blessed are the dead that the rain falls on,' and later calls Gatsby 'the poor son-of-a-bitch.' The narrator, reflecting on his Midwestern roots during a Christmas train ride to Chicago, realizes he and his circle—Tom, Daisy, Jordan, Gatsby—are all Westerners fundamentally unfit for the East, which he now sees as a distorted, haunting landscape like an El Greco painting. He meets Jordan Baker, who reveals she is engaged to another man; he confronts her about her dishonesty and admits he is too old to lie to himself, and they part with unresolved emotion. On a late October afternoon, Nick encounters Tom Buchanan on Fifth Avenue, who admits he told Wilson that Gatsby owned the car that killed Myrtle, justifying it as self-defense and weeping over dog biscuits left in his flat. Nick, unable to forgive him, recognizes Tom and Daisy as careless people who destroy lives and retreat into wealth; he shakes Tom’s hand, feeling as if speaking to a child, as Tom enters a jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace or cuff buttons. On his final night in New York, the narrator walks to Gatsby’s overgrown mansion, erases an obscene word scrawled on the steps with his shoe, and wanders to the beach, reflecting on Gatsby’s futile dream of reclaiming the past through Daisy, culminating in the iconic image of boats beating against the current, borne back ceaselessly into the past.</div>\n1072:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n1076:                    <span class=\"tag\">Wilson</span>\n1112:                <h3>Main Characters <span class=\"count\">11</span></h3>\n1117:                        Gatsby\n1127:                    <div class=\"aliases-list\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\">Also known as: Mr. Gatsby</div>\n1142:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Gatsby is a mysterious and enigmatic figure whose life is defined by an obsessive pursuit of a past ideal, symbolized by the green light across the bay. He presents a carefully constructed persona of wealth and extravagance, hosting lavish parties to attract attention and ultimately reconnect with Daisy, yet remains emotionally isolated and deeply vulnerable beneath his public facade.</div>\n1159:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby is intensely focused on a distant goal symbolized by a green light</strong></div>\n1171:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby maintains a formal and detached presence despite hosting chaotic parties</strong></div>\n1183:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby is emotionally overwhelmed and desperate in private moments</strong></div>\n1195:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby is deeply affected by Daisy's emotional state and seeks to control the narrative around her</strong></div>\n1207:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby’s wealth and lifestyle are tied to a fabricated identity and possibly illicit activities</strong></div>\n1219:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby is ultimately isolated and misunderstood, even in death</strong></div>\n1231:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby’s dream is rooted in a romantic ideal that transcends reality</strong></div>\n1281:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Tom Buchanan is a wealthy, dominant figure in East Egg who exhibits a sense of entitlement and control in both personal and social interactions. He displays intellectual pretension, particularly in defending racial hierarchies, and reacts with irritation to perceived challenges to his authority, especially during confrontations involving Daisy and Gatsby.</div>\n1323:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Daisy Buchanan is a woman of apparent fragility and charm, observed by the narrator as both emotionally vulnerable and socially poised. Her interactions reveal a complex mix of whimsy, vulnerability, and emotional detachment, particularly in moments of tension with her husband Tom and during her encounters with Gatsby. Over time, her behavior shifts from playful evasion to visible fear and helplessness, culminating in a moment of decisive action that underscores her entrapment within her social world.</div>\n1376:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Daisy is emotionally affected by Gatsby’s presence and the confrontation with Tom</strong></div>\n1447:                    <div class=\"aliases-list\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\">Also known as: George B. Wilson, George Wilson, Myrtle Wilson, George, Mrs. Wilson</div>\n1462:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Myrtle Wilson is a woman who seeks escape from her constrained life through relationships and displays of social aspiration. She exhibits a shift in demeanor when in the presence of Tom Buchanan, adopting an exaggerated sense of refinement, yet reveals vulnerability and insecurity in her personal history and relationships.</div>\n1515:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle claims she married George Wilson believing he was a gentleman</strong></div>\n1678:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>She is involved in facilitating communication between Gatsby and Nick, acting as an intermediary</strong></div>\n1690:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>She is described as a 'great sportswoman' by Gatsby, suggesting a connection to athletic or competitive activities</strong></div>\n1752:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Meyer Wolfshiem is a powerful and morally corrupt figure whose actions have directly enabled criminal enterprises, including the fixing of the 1919 World Series and the murder of Rosy Rosenthal. He maintains a facade of charm and loyalty to Gatsby, but ultimately abandons him in death, refusing to attend the funeral out of fear of being 'mixed up' in death, revealing his self-serving and cowardly nature.</div>\n2016:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Nick is a reflective observer who serves as a peripheral yet central figure in the events of the narrative. He is introduced as a newcomer to West Egg, maintaining close ties with his cousin Daisy and her husband Tom, while also forming connections with other characters like Jordan Baker and Jay Gatsby. Over time, he becomes increasingly aware of the emotional and moral complexities around him, particularly during pivotal moments involving Daisy and Gatsby.</div>\n2081:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Nick is involved in a moment of emotional tension between Daisy and Gatsby</strong></div>\n2185:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Sloane is a man who exhibits haughty, dismissive behavior and actively undermines Gatsby's attempts to connect, demonstrating a deliberate exclusion of Gatsby from social interactions. His actions, including whispering to the woman in the brown riding-habit and coercing her to leave without Gatsby, reveal a manipulative and antagonistic nature that serves to humiliate and isolate Gatsby.</div>\n2202:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Sloane displays haughtiness and disinterest in Gatsby's hospitality</strong></div>\n2214:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Sloane refuses to allow Gatsby to join their ride, asserting control over the group</strong></div>\n2226:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Sloane actively prevents Gatsby from participating in the social event</strong></div>\n2238:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Sloane's behavior is coordinated with Tom Buchanan to marginalize Gatsby</strong></div>\n2286:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Catherine is a worldly, observant woman in her early thirties, portrayed as both perceptive and complicit in maintaining social illusions. She displays a sharp awareness of others' relationships and emotional dynamics, yet participates in fabricating narratives that obscure the truth, particularly in the aftermath of Gatsby’s death. Her actions reveal a complex moral stance—she offers insight while also enabling deception, aligning her with both harm and protection.</div>\n2327:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>She fabricates a false narrative about Myrtle’s knowledge of Gatsby to simplify the story after his death</strong></div>\n2391:                <h3>Supporting Characters <span class=\"count\">30</span></h3>\n2421:                                <div class=\"description\">Jay Gatsby, originally James Gatz, is a self-made man whose identity was forged through ambition and a transformative encounter with Dan Cody. He exhibits both admirable determination and moral ambigu...</div>\n2460:                                <div class=\"description\">James Gatz, who transformed himself into Jay Gatsby, is a man driven by ambition and a desire to transcend his humble origins. His journey from a poor North Dakota farm boy to a wealthy self-made figu...</div>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6595f10d-dd12-40e9-9d88-7f5d63eb6af8","uuid":"24b30e68-1c40-4ddc-9e80-b4febd88c3ed","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"7:    <title>The Great Gatsby - Audiobook Prep Report</title>\n574:                <h1>The Great Gatsby</h1>\n611:                    <div class=\"label\">Main Characters</div>\n631:                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Mrs. Sigourney Howard, a reflective and observant woman, recounts her experiences in the summer of 1922, set against the glittering yet hollow backdrop of Long Island’s East and West Egg. She begins her story with her arrival in West Egg, where she rents a modest home and quickly becomes entangled in the lives of her cousin Daisy Buchanan and her domineering husband Tom, a man whose racist rhetoric and emotional cruelty unsettle her. Her introduction to the enigmatic Jay Gatsby occurs on a fateful night when she sees him standing on the lawn, arms outstretched toward a distant green light across the bay—a symbol of his unattainable dream. This moment marks the beginning of her deepening involvement with Gatsby, whose lavish parties and mysterious past intrigue her, even as she remains wary of the illusions he cultivates.\n633:As the summer unfolds, Mrs. Howard becomes increasingly drawn into the tangled web of relationships that define the era’s elite. She witnesses Tom’s affair with Myrtle Wilson, a vibrant but desperate woman whose life is crushed by the very system she seeks to escape. The revelation of Gatsby’s past—his real name, James Gatz, and his rise from poverty through a series of calculated reinventions—unfolds gradually, revealing his obsessive love for Daisy, whom he has idealized since their brief romance years before. Gatsby’s dream of rekindling their relationship is tested when Daisy, under pressure from Tom, confesses she never truly loved him, shattering Gatsby’s illusion. The emotional climax occurs at the Plaza Hotel, where Gatsby’s dream is publicly dismantled, and the fatal accident involving Myrtle Wilson follows, with Daisy driving the car that kills her.\n635:In the aftermath, Mrs. Howard grapples with the moral decay and emotional emptiness of the world she once admired. Gatsby, believing he must protect Daisy, takes the blame for the accident, only to be murdered by George Wilson, who has been manipulated into believing Gatsby was responsible. The funeral, attended by only a few, underscores Gatsby’s profound isolation despite his wealth. Mrs. Howard reflects on the futility of chasing an idealized past, recognizing that Gatsby’s dream was not about Daisy herself, but about a version of himself that could never exist. In her final moments, she walks through the overgrown grounds of Gatsby’s mansion, erasing a vulgar inscription and contemplating the tragic inevitability of the American Dream’s collapse—where ambition, identity, and love are ultimately consumed by the relentless current of time and societal indifference.</div>\n798:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental in West Egg, feeling a newfound sense of belonging after helping a stranger find the village; he observes the opulent mansions of West Egg and East Egg, then drives across the bay to dine with his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom at their grand Georgian Colonial mansion in East Egg, where he meets Jordan Baker and is struck by Tom’s domineering presence and Daisy’s fragile charm. During a tense evening on the rosy-coloured porch at dusk, with four candles flickering despite Daisy’s objection, Tom passionately defends white supremacy using The Rise of the Coloured Empires, while Daisy mocks his pretensions, shares a whimsical secret about the butler’s nose, and later confides in Nick with startling vulnerability, declaring she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool'; after Tom receives a mysterious phone call and returns, Daisy feigns enchantment with a nightingale to deflect attention, and the evening ends with Tom and Jordan retreating to the library while Nick and Daisy sit in silent, uneasy communion. Later, as Nick departs by car, Daisy calls out to him to wait, and upon stopping in his yard at West Egg, he sees a mysterious figure—later identified as Gatsby—standing alone on the lawn, arms outstretched toward the dark water, trembling as he gazes at a single green light at the end of a distant dock, before vanishing into the night.</div>\n812:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n827:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a summer Sunday afternoon, Tom Buchanan forcibly removes the narrator from a train stopped in the desolate Valley of Ashes, where they meet Myrtle Wilson at her husband George B. Wilson’s run-down garage, which houses only a dusty Ford wreck; Tom and Myrtle arrange to meet in New York, with Myrtle taking a separate train car to avoid detection, and she purchases an Airedale dog for $10 from a man resembling John D. Rockefeller. Arriving at Myrtle’s top-floor apartment at 158th Street, they find it cluttered with oversized tapestried furniture and a blurred photograph mistakenly identified as a hen but later revealed to be Myrtle’s mother; Tom opens whisky from a locked bureau, and Myrtle calls her sister Catherine and the McKees, who arrive as she changes into a cream-coloured chiffon dress, transforming her demeanor into exaggerated hauteur. The party descends into chaos when Myrtle, emboldened by alcohol and the pretense of sophistication, repeatedly shouts Daisy’s name, prompting Tom to violently break her nose, after which the narrator, exhausted and disturbed, leaves the apartment and spends the night sleeping in the cold waiting room of Pennsylvania Station.</div>\n837:                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n839:                    <span class=\"tag\">George B. Wilson</span>\n858:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator arriving by car at Gatsby’s mansion in West Egg on a Saturday evening, invited formally by a handwritten note delivered by a chauffeur in a robin’s-egg-blue uniform, only to find himself among a vast, chaotic crowd of uninvited guests arriving from New York by automobile. The garden erupts in revelry with a full orchestra, lavish buffets, dancing under colored lights, and rampant rumors about Gatsby’s past—including claims he killed a man or was a German spy—while the narrator wanders through the excess, meeting Jordan Baker and encountering eccentric guests such as the owl-eyed man who marvels at the authenticity of Gatsby’s uncut books. As the party peaks at midnight with opera and champagne, Gatsby remains unseen until the narrator, after drinking champagne, unexpectedly converses with him, learning of his recent hydroplane purchase and an invitation to fly along the Sound the next morning; amid growing drunken chaos—marital fights, a woman weeping black tears from mascara, and a car crash outside where a coupé loses a wheel and two bewildered men claim innocence—Gatsby stands apart, sober and formal, and later summons Jordan Baker alone, leaving her visibly shaken. The chapter closes with the narrator reflecting on his daily life in New York: commuting on foot to the Probity Trust, observing lonely young clerks at twilight, romanticizing strangers on Fifth Avenue, and rekindling a complex relationship with Jordan, whose dishonesty—evident in her lies about a borrowed car and a past golf scandal—fails to provoke moral outrage, even as he remains entangled in letters to a woman back home, haunted by memories of her perspiration on her lip during tennis.</div>\n864:                    <span class=\"tag\">Jay Gatsby</span>\n893:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a Sunday morning as church bells ring along the shore, Gatsby’s mansion becomes the center of a chaotic, glittering social scene filled with guests from East Egg, West Egg, and New York. The narrator recalls a detailed list of attendees—many of whom are wealthy, eccentric, or morally dubious—arriving by automobile, including the Chester Beckers, the Blackbucks, the Snells, the Dancies, and numerous figures connected to the film industry and organized crime. Guests mingle carelessly, spreading wild rumors about Gatsby’s past, such as his alleged ties to Von Hindenburg and the devil, while indulging in cocktails and flowers. Some, like Klipspringer, become regulars known as 'the boarder,' while others, like Ripley Snell, are already on the brink of ruin, having been run over by a car on Gatsby’s gravel drive. The atmosphere is one of superficial glamour masking moral decay and profound ignorance of the host.</div>\n928:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a July morning, Gatsby arrives at the narrator’s door in his luxurious cream-colored car with a three-note horn, inviting him to lunch and launching an elaborate, inconsistent account of his past—including Oxford education, European aristocratic life, and wartime heroism in Montenegro, backed by a medal and photograph. During the drive through West Egg, the valley of ashes, and across the Queensboro Bridge into New York City, Gatsby uses a white card to evade a traffic stop, hinting at hidden influence. At noon in a cellar on Forty-second Street, the narrator meets Meyer Wolfshiem, a grotesque figure with human molar cuff buttons, who recounts the 1919 murder of Rosy Rosenthal and reveals Gatsby fixed the 1919 World Series, shocking the narrator. Later, Jordan Baker reveals to Nick that Gatsby bought his mansion solely to be near Daisy, and arranges a tea meeting; on a rainy day, Gatsby sends a man to cut Nick’s grass and delivers a greenhouse of flowers. When Daisy arrives in a large open car, Gatsby is pale and trembling, nearly knocking over a clock, and their reunion is initially stiff and despairing until Nick leaves them alone, after which Gatsby shows Daisy his mansion, his collection of expensive shirts that move her to tears, and his opulent rooms including a gold toilet set and Merton College Library, restoring his belief in his dream. As the storm clears, Gatsby points to the green light at Daisy’s dock, now diminished in meaning, and presents clippings about her and a photograph of his mentor Dan Cody, while Klipspringer plays a poorly executed piano piece; Gatsby’s illusion of Daisy begins to crack as he becomes utterly absorbed in her voice, forgetting the narrator entirely.</div>\n934:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n963:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with a New York reporter arriving at Gatsby’s West Egg mansion seeking a statement, drawn by rumors that Gatsby lives in a secret boat disguised as a house, reflecting how his notoriety has spiraled into urban legend; the narrative then shifts to Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz, a poor North Dakota farm boy who, at seventeen, rowed out to Dan Cody’s yacht anchored off Lake Superior, adopted the name Jay Gatsby, and spent five years aboard the Tuolomee serving in roles from steward to jailor, profoundly shaped by Cody’s wealth and lifestyle—only to be cheated out of a $25,000 inheritance by Ella Kaye after Cody’s death, leaving him with nothing but the refined persona he had cultivated. Weeks later, the narrator visits Gatsby’s mansion on a Sunday afternoon when Tom Buchanan, Mr. Sloane, and a woman in a brown riding-habit arrive on horseback; Gatsby nervously offers them drinks and invites them to supper, but they subtly rebuff his hospitality and refuse to let him join their ride back. Later, Tom and Daisy attend one of Gatsby’s parties, where Daisy is both fascinated and repulsed by the chaotic, vulgar energy of West Egg, dances with Gatsby, but Tom grows increasingly suspicious and dismissive, ultimately declaring him a bootlegger; afterward, Gatsby confides in the narrator that Daisy didn’t enjoy herself and clings to the delusion that he can repeat the past, specifically that Daisy will tell Tom she never loved him so they can return to Louisville and restart their relationship as if five years had never passed, recalling the moment he first kissed her under the moonlight as the origin of his idealized vision.</div>\n969:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n998:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house, where Gatsby has dismissed his servants and replaced them with Wolfsheim’s relatives; during a tense lunch, Daisy kisses Gatsby and declares her love in front of Tom, who becomes alarmed and later reveals he has investigated Gatsby’s past, mocking his Oxford claim while Gatsby observes Daisy’s voice is 'full of money.' The group departs for New York in two cars—Gatsby driving his own yellow vehicle, Tom taking the blue coupé—and after a heated ride, they stop at Wilson’s garage in the Valley of Ashes, where Wilson, ill and desperate, reveals he plans to take Myrtle away, while Myrtle, mistaking Jordan for Tom’s wife, watches in jealous terror under the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg. At the Plaza Hotel, Tom confronts Gatsby with accusations of bootlegging via drugstores tied to Wolfsheim, and under pressure, Daisy admits she once loved Tom, shattering Gatsby’s idealized dream; Tom orders them to leave in Gatsby’s car, and they depart in silence. Later, Myrtle rushes out of the garage at dusk and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that flees, witnessed by Michaelis, who is unsure of its exact color; Tom and the narrator arrive at the scene to find Myrtle’s body covered on a worktable, Wilson hysterical, and Tom urgently denies involvement, claiming his blue coupé was not the car and later breaks down in tears, calling the driver a 'God damned coward.' That night, Nick waits outside the Buchanan house and finds Gatsby lurking in the shadows, who confesses Daisy was driving the fatal car but intends to take the blame and will wait outside her window all night; Nick sees through the pantry window that Tom and Daisy are quietly together in the kitchen, already reconciled, and Gatsby stands alone in the moonlight, vigilantly watching a house where his dream has already ended.</div>\n1006:                    <span class=\"tag\">Jay Gatsby</span>\n1014:                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n1016:                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n1031:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter unfolds across a single day and night, beginning with the narrator’s sleepless night haunted by a foghorn and his frantic rush to Gatsby’s mansion at dawn after hearing a taxi arrive, where he finds Gatsby dejected after seeing Daisy briefly appear at her window before turning off the light. Inside the vast, dusty, and neglected mansion, they search blindly for cigarettes and smoke on the open French windows of the drawing-room, during which Gatsby refuses to flee to Atlantic City or Montreal, insisting he must know Daisy’s fate, and reveals his past as a penniless officer who misled her into believing he was her social equal, binding himself to a dream she abandoned after their brief romance. Later, the narrator reflects on his own final afternoon with Daisy in a warm room filled with fire, where their silent intimacy and tender gestures—Daisy brushing her lips against his coat’s shoulder, he touching her fingers—reveal a love that eclipsed his ambitions, a moment deliberately crafted as a farewell before his departure. Meanwhile, George Wilson, shattered by Myrtle’s death, fixates on the yellow car and the dog-leash found in her bureau, declaring 'He murdered her' and later staring at the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg, muttering 'God sees everything' as he descends into religious delusion; after vanishing for hours, he is traced walking from his garage to Port Roosevelt, then to Gad’s Hill where he buys an uneaten sandwich and coffee, before arriving in West Egg by half-past two and asking for Gatsby’s house. At two o'clock, Gatsby dons his bathing suit, instructs the butler to bring calls to the pool, and carries the old pneumatic mattress alone to the pool, having stopped the chauffeur from using the damaged car; the butler waits until four a.m. for a call that never comes, signaling Gatsby’s final loss of hope. Gunshots are heard, and the narrator rushes to find Gatsby’s body floating in the pool, the mattress drifting with faint ripples, moments before the gardener discovers Wilson’s body in the grass, completing the tragedy. The narrator, later in his city office, attempts to call Gatsby four times only to find the line busy with a long-distance call from Detroit, and after a tense, abrupt phone call with Jordan Baker—who accuses him of being unfriendly and leaves for Southampton by car—he marks the 3:50 train and sits in silence, visibly unsettled.</div>\n1039:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n1045:                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n1066:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">In the aftermath of Gatsby’s death, his mansion is overrun by police, photographers, and reporters, while the coroner’s dismissal of Wilson as a 'madman' and Catherine’s false testimony simplify the narrative into a tale of grief-driven violence. The narrator, feeling sole responsibility for Gatsby’s isolation, desperately tries to reach Daisy and Meyer Wolfshiem—calling Information, sending a letter via butler, and waiting for a wire—but receives no response, only a disconnected long-distance call from Chicago that mistakenly believed Gatsby was alive. On the third day, Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives by train from Minnesota, visibly shaken and bundled in a cheap ulster, displaying a worn photograph of the mansion and a childhood book with a handwritten self-improvement schedule, revealing Gatsby’s early ambition. Only Gatz and a Lutheran minister attend the funeral in a drizzling rain, as Klipspringer calls to request his tennis shoes and Wolfshiem refuses to come, citing fear of being 'mixed up' in death. At the cemetery, the man with owl-eyed glasses, drenched and wiping rain from his spectacles, arrives unexpectedly, murmurs 'Amen to that' after hearing 'Blessed are the dead that the rain falls on,' and later calls Gatsby 'the poor son-of-a-bitch.' The narrator, reflecting on his Midwestern roots during a Christmas train ride to Chicago, realizes he and his circle—Tom, Daisy, Jordan, Gatsby—are all Westerners fundamentally unfit for the East, which he now sees as a distorted, haunting landscape like an El Greco painting. He meets Jordan Baker, who reveals she is engaged to another man; he confronts her about her dishonesty and admits he is too old to lie to himself, and they part with unresolved emotion. On a late October afternoon, Nick encounters Tom Buchanan on Fifth Avenue, who admits he told Wilson that Gatsby owned the car that killed Myrtle, justifying it as self-defense and weeping over dog biscuits left in his flat. Nick, unable to forgive him, recognizes Tom and Daisy as careless people who destroy lives and retreat into wealth; he shakes Tom’s hand, feeling as if speaking to a child, as Tom enters a jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace or cuff buttons. On his final night in New York, the narrator walks to Gatsby’s overgrown mansion, erases an obscene word scrawled on the steps with his shoe, and wanders to the beach, reflecting on Gatsby’s futile dream of reclaiming the past through Daisy, culminating in the iconic image of boats beating against the current, borne back ceaselessly into the past.</div>\n1072:                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n1076:                    <span class=\"tag\">Wilson</span>\n1112:                <h3>Main Characters <span class=\"count\">11</span></h3>\n1117:                        Gatsby\n1127:                    <div class=\"aliases-list\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\">Also known as: Mr. Gatsby</div>\n1142:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Gatsby is a mysterious and enigmatic figure whose life is defined by an obsessive pursuit of a past ideal, symbolized by the green light across the bay. He presents a carefully constructed persona of wealth and extravagance, hosting lavish parties to attract attention and ultimately reconnect with Daisy, yet remains emotionally isolated and deeply vulnerable beneath his public facade.</div>\n1159:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby is intensely focused on a distant goal symbolized by a green light</strong></div>\n1171:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby maintains a formal and detached presence despite hosting chaotic parties</strong></div>\n1183:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby is emotionally overwhelmed and desperate in private moments</strong></div>\n1195:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby is deeply affected by Daisy's emotional state and seeks to control the narrative around her</strong></div>\n1207:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby’s wealth and lifestyle are tied to a fabricated identity and possibly illicit activities</strong></div>\n1219:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby is ultimately isolated and misunderstood, even in death</strong></div>\n1231:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Gatsby’s dream is rooted in a romantic ideal that transcends reality</strong></div>\n1281:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Tom Buchanan is a wealthy, dominant figure in East Egg who exhibits a sense of entitlement and control in both personal and social interactions. He displays intellectual pretension, particularly in defending racial hierarchies, and reacts with irritation to perceived challenges to his authority, especially during confrontations involving Daisy and Gatsby.</div>\n1323:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Daisy Buchanan is a woman of apparent fragility and charm, observed by the narrator as both emotionally vulnerable and socially poised. Her interactions reveal a complex mix of whimsy, vulnerability, and emotional detachment, particularly in moments of tension with her husband Tom and during her encounters with Gatsby. Over time, her behavior shifts from playful evasion to visible fear and helplessness, culminating in a moment of decisive action that underscores her entrapment within her social world.</div>\n1376:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Daisy is emotionally affected by Gatsby’s presence and the confrontation with Tom</strong></div>\n1447:                    <div class=\"aliases-list\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\">Also known as: George B. Wilson, George Wilson, Myrtle Wilson, George, Mrs. Wilson</div>\n1462:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Myrtle Wilson is a woman who seeks escape from her constrained life through relationships and displays of social aspiration. She exhibits a shift in demeanor when in the presence of Tom Buchanan, adopting an exaggerated sense of refinement, yet reveals vulnerability and insecurity in her personal history and relationships.</div>\n1515:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle claims she married George Wilson believing he was a gentleman</strong></div>\n1678:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>She is involved in facilitating communication between Gatsby and Nick, acting as an intermediary</strong></div>\n1690:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>She is described as a 'great sportswoman' by Gatsby, suggesting a connection to athletic or competitive activities</strong></div>\n1752:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Meyer Wolfshiem is a powerful and morally corrupt figure whose actions have directly enabled criminal enterprises, including the fixing of the 1919 World Series and the murder of Rosy Rosenthal. He maintains a facade of charm and loyalty to Gatsby, but ultimately abandons him in death, refusing to attend the funeral out of fear of being 'mixed up' in death, revealing his self-serving and cowardly nature.</div>\n2016:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Nick is a reflective observer who serves as a peripheral yet central figure in the events of the narrative. He is introduced as a newcomer to West Egg, maintaining close ties with his cousin Daisy and her husband Tom, while also forming connections with other characters like Jordan Baker and Jay Gatsby. Over time, he becomes increasingly aware of the emotional and moral complexities around him, particularly during pivotal moments involving Daisy and Gatsby.</div>\n2081:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Nick is involved in a moment of emotional tension between Daisy and Gatsby</strong></div>\n2185:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Sloane is a man who exhibits haughty, dismissive behavior and actively undermines Gatsby's attempts to connect, demonstrating a deliberate exclusion of Gatsby from social interactions. His actions, including whispering to the woman in the brown riding-habit and coercing her to leave without Gatsby, reveal a manipulative and antagonistic nature that serves to humiliate and isolate Gatsby.</div>\n2202:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Sloane displays haughtiness and disinterest in Gatsby's hospitality</strong></div>\n2214:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Sloane refuses to allow Gatsby to join their ride, asserting control over the group</strong></div>\n2226:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Sloane actively prevents Gatsby from participating in the social event</strong></div>\n2238:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Sloane's behavior is coordinated with Tom Buchanan to marginalize Gatsby</strong></div>\n2286:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Catherine is a worldly, observant woman in her early thirties, portrayed as both perceptive and complicit in maintaining social illusions. She displays a sharp awareness of others' relationships and emotional dynamics, yet participates in fabricating narratives that obscure the truth, particularly in the aftermath of Gatsby’s death. Her actions reveal a complex moral stance—she offers insight while also enabling deception, aligning her with both harm and protection.</div>\n2327:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>She fabricates a false narrative about Myrtle’s knowledge of Gatsby to simplify the story after his death</strong></div>\n2391:                <h3>Supporting Characters <span class=\"count\">30</span></h3>\n2421:                                <div class=\"description\">Jay Gatsby, originally James Gatz, is a self-made man whose identity was forged through ambition and a transformative encounter with Dan Cody. He exhibits both admirable determination and moral ambigu...</div>\n2460:                                <div class=\"description\">James Gatz, who transformed himself into Jay Gatsby, is a man driven by ambition and a desire to transcend his humble origins. His journey from a poor North Dakota farm boy to a wealthy self-made figu...</div>","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
