{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WQpkFpJwNk9UEUmajCDUoq","type":"tool_result","content":"                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental in West Egg, feeling a newfound sense of belonging after helping a stranger find the village; he observes the opulent mansions of West Egg and East Egg, then drives across the bay to dine with his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom at their grand Georgian Colonial mansion in East Egg, where he meets Jordan Baker and is struck by Tom’s domineering presence and Daisy’s fragile charm. During a tense evening on the rosy-coloured porch at dusk, with four candles flickering despite Daisy’s objection, Tom passionately defends white supremacy using The Rise of the Coloured Empires, while Daisy mocks his pretensions, shares a whimsical secret about the butler’s nose, and later confides in Nick with startling vulnerability, declaring she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool'; after Tom receives a mysterious phone call and returns, Daisy feigns enchantment with a nightingale to deflect attention, and the evening ends with Tom and Jordan retreating to the library while Nick and Daisy sit in silent, uneasy communion. Later, as Nick departs by car, Daisy calls out to him to wait, and upon stopping in his yard at West Egg, he sees a mysterious figure—later identified as Gatsby—standing alone on the lawn, arms outstretched toward the dark water, trembling as he gazes at a single green light at the end of a distant dock, before vanishing into the night.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">the butler</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 2: II</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">4,280 words &middot; 28m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a summer Sunday afternoon, Tom Buchanan forcibly removes the narrator from a train stopped in the desolate Valley of Ashes, where they meet Myrtle Wilson at her husband George B. Wilson’s run-down garage, which houses only a dusty Ford wreck; Tom and Myrtle arrange to meet in New York, with Myrtle taking a separate train car to avoid detection, and she purchases an Airedale dog for $10 from a man resembling John D. Rockefeller. Arriving at Myrtle’s top-floor apartment at 158th Street, they find it cluttered with oversized tapestried furniture and a blurred photograph mistakenly identified as a hen but later revealed to be Myrtle’s mother; Tom opens whisky from a locked bureau, and Myrtle calls her sister Catherine and the McKees, who arrive as she changes into a cream-coloured chiffon dress, transforming her demeanor into exaggerated hauteur. The party descends into chaos when Myrtle, emboldened by alcohol and the pretense of sophistication, repeatedly shouts Daisy’s name, prompting Tom to violently break her nose, after which the narrator, exhausted and disturbed, leaves the apartment and spends the night sleeping in the cold waiting room of Pennsylvania Station.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">the narrator</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6595f10d-dd12-40e9-9d88-7f5d63eb6af8","uuid":"b2d431ef-4db5-463f-9692-f6413a54915c","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental in West Egg, feeling a newfound sense of belonging after helping a stranger find the village; he observes the opulent mansions of West Egg and East Egg, then drives across the bay to dine with his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom at their grand Georgian Colonial mansion in East Egg, where he meets Jordan Baker and is struck by Tom’s domineering presence and Daisy’s fragile charm. During a tense evening on the rosy-coloured porch at dusk, with four candles flickering despite Daisy’s objection, Tom passionately defends white supremacy using The Rise of the Coloured Empires, while Daisy mocks his pretensions, shares a whimsical secret about the butler’s nose, and later confides in Nick with startling vulnerability, declaring she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool'; after Tom receives a mysterious phone call and returns, Daisy feigns enchantment with a nightingale to deflect attention, and the evening ends with Tom and Jordan retreating to the library while Nick and Daisy sit in silent, uneasy communion. Later, as Nick departs by car, Daisy calls out to him to wait, and upon stopping in his yard at West Egg, he sees a mysterious figure—later identified as Gatsby—standing alone on the lawn, arms outstretched toward the dark water, trembling as he gazes at a single green light at the end of a distant dock, before vanishing into the night.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">the butler</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 2: II</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">4,280 words &middot; 28m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a summer Sunday afternoon, Tom Buchanan forcibly removes the narrator from a train stopped in the desolate Valley of Ashes, where they meet Myrtle Wilson at her husband George B. Wilson’s run-down garage, which houses only a dusty Ford wreck; Tom and Myrtle arrange to meet in New York, with Myrtle taking a separate train car to avoid detection, and she purchases an Airedale dog for $10 from a man resembling John D. Rockefeller. Arriving at Myrtle’s top-floor apartment at 158th Street, they find it cluttered with oversized tapestried furniture and a blurred photograph mistakenly identified as a hen but later revealed to be Myrtle’s mother; Tom opens whisky from a locked bureau, and Myrtle calls her sister Catherine and the McKees, who arrive as she changes into a cream-coloured chiffon dress, transforming her demeanor into exaggerated hauteur. The party descends into chaos when Myrtle, emboldened by alcohol and the pretense of sophistication, repeatedly shouts Daisy’s name, prompting Tom to violently break her nose, after which the narrator, exhausted and disturbed, leaves the apartment and spends the night sleeping in the cold waiting room of Pennsylvania Station.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">the narrator</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
