{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016evKQ7toSS2GQTbqXVTw4h","type":"tool_result","content":"798:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental in West Egg, feeling a newfound sense of belonging after helping a stranger find the village; he observes the opulent mansions of West Egg and East Egg, then drives across the bay to dine with his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom at their grand Georgian Colonial mansion in East Egg, where he meets Jordan Baker and is struck by Tom’s domineering presence and Daisy’s fragile charm. During a tense evening on the rosy-coloured porch at dusk, with four candles flickering despite Daisy’s objection, Tom passionately defends white supremacy using The Rise of the Coloured Empires, while Daisy mocks his pretensions, shares a whimsical secret about the butler’s nose, and later confides in Nick with startling vulnerability, declaring she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool'; after Tom receives a mysterious phone call and returns, Daisy feigns enchantment with a nightingale to deflect attention, and the evening ends with Tom and Jordan retreating to the library while Nick and Daisy sit in silent, uneasy communion. Later, as Nick departs by car, Daisy calls out to him to wait, and upon stopping in his yard at West Egg, he sees a mysterious figure—later identified as Gatsby—standing alone on the lawn, arms outstretched toward the dark water, trembling as he gazes at a single green light at the end of a distant dock, before vanishing into the night.</div>\n810:                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n858:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator arriving by car at Gatsby’s mansion in West Egg on a Saturday evening, invited formally by a handwritten note delivered by a chauffeur in a robin’s-egg-blue uniform, only to find himself among a vast, chaotic crowd of uninvited guests arriving from New York by automobile. The garden erupts in revelry with a full orchestra, lavish buffets, dancing under colored lights, and rampant rumors about Gatsby’s past—including claims he killed a man or was a German spy—while the narrator wanders through the excess, meeting Jordan Baker and encountering eccentric guests such as the owl-eyed man who marvels at the authenticity of Gatsby’s uncut books. As the party peaks at midnight with opera and champagne, Gatsby remains unseen until the narrator, after drinking champagne, unexpectedly converses with him, learning of his recent hydroplane purchase and an invitation to fly along the Sound the next morning; amid growing drunken chaos—marital fights, a woman weeping black tears from mascara, and a car crash outside where a coupé loses a wheel and two bewildered men claim innocence—Gatsby stands apart, sober and formal, and later summons Jordan Baker alone, leaving her visibly shaken. The chapter closes with the narrator reflecting on his daily life in New York: commuting on foot to the Probity Trust, observing lonely young clerks at twilight, romanticizing strangers on Fifth Avenue, and rekindling a complex relationship with Jordan, whose dishonesty—evident in her lies about a borrowed car and a past golf scandal—fails to provoke moral outrage, even as he remains entangled in letters to a woman back home, haunted by memories of her perspiration on her lip during tennis.</div>\n868:                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n928:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a July morning, Gatsby arrives at the narrator’s door in his luxurious cream-colored car with a three-note horn, inviting him to lunch and launching an elaborate, inconsistent account of his past—including Oxford education, European aristocratic life, and wartime heroism in Montenegro, backed by a medal and photograph. During the drive through West Egg, the valley of ashes, and across the Queensboro Bridge into New York City, Gatsby uses a white card to evade a traffic stop, hinting at hidden influence. At noon in a cellar on Forty-second Street, the narrator meets Meyer Wolfshiem, a grotesque figure with human molar cuff buttons, who recounts the 1919 murder of Rosy Rosenthal and reveals Gatsby fixed the 1919 World Series, shocking the narrator. Later, Jordan Baker reveals to Nick that Gatsby bought his mansion solely to be near Daisy, and arranges a tea meeting; on a rainy day, Gatsby sends a man to cut Nick’s grass and delivers a greenhouse of flowers. When Daisy arrives in a large open car, Gatsby is pale and trembling, nearly knocking over a clock, and their reunion is initially stiff and despairing until Nick leaves them alone, after which Gatsby shows Daisy his mansion, his collection of expensive shirts that move her to tears, and his opulent rooms including a gold toilet set and Merton College Library, restoring his belief in his dream. As the storm clears, Gatsby points to the green light at Daisy’s dock, now diminished in meaning, and presents clippings about her and a photograph of his mentor Dan Cody, while Klipspringer plays a poorly executed piano piece; Gatsby’s illusion of Daisy begins to crack as he becomes utterly absorbed in her voice, forgetting the narrator entirely.</div>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6595f10d-dd12-40e9-9d88-7f5d63eb6af8","uuid":"b157c115-1654-4f68-972c-070af47dc8f2","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"798:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental in West Egg, feeling a newfound sense of belonging after helping a stranger find the village; he observes the opulent mansions of West Egg and East Egg, then drives across the bay to dine with his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom at their grand Georgian Colonial mansion in East Egg, where he meets Jordan Baker and is struck by Tom’s d