{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BDYboZjopJbqtqrTtr57Wt","type":"tool_result","content":"As the summer unfolds, Mrs. Howard becomes increasingly drawn into the tangled web of relationships that define the era’s elite. She witnesses Tom’s affair with Myrtle Wilson, a vibrant but desperate woman whose life is crushed by the very system she seeks to escape. The revelation of Gatsby’s past—his real name, James Gatz, and his rise from poverty through a series of calculated reinventions—unfolds gradually, revealing his obsessive love for Daisy, whom he has idealized since their brief romance years before. Gatsby’s dream of rekindling their relationship is tested when Daisy, under pressure from Tom, confesses she never truly loved him, shattering Gatsby’s illusion. The emotional climax occurs at the Plaza Hotel, where Gatsby’s dream is publicly dismantled, and the fatal accident involving Myrtle Wilson follows, with Daisy driving the car that kills her.\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental in West Egg, feeling a newfound sense of belonging after helping a stranger find the village; he observes the opulent mansions of West Egg and East Egg, then drives across the bay to dine with his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom at their grand Georgian Colonial mansion in East Egg, where he meets Jordan Baker and is struck by Tom’s domineering presence and Daisy’s fragile charm. During a tense evening on the rosy-coloured porch at dusk, with four candles flickering despite Daisy’s objection, Tom passionately defends white supremacy using The Rise of the Coloured Empires, while Daisy mocks his pretensions, shares a whimsical secret about the butler’s nose, and later confides in Nick with startling vulnerability, declaring she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool'; after Tom receives a mysterious phone call and returns, Daisy feigns enchantment with a nightingale to deflect attention, and the evening ends with Tom and Jordan retreating to the library while Nick and Daisy sit in silent, uneasy communion. Later, as Nick departs by car, Daisy calls out to him to wait, and upon stopping in his yard at West Egg, he sees a mysterious figure—later identified as Gatsby—standing alone on the lawn, arms outstretched toward the dark water, trembling as he gazes at a single green light at the end of a distant dock, before vanishing into the night.</div>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house, where Gatsby has dismissed his servants and replaced them with Wolfsheim’s relatives; during a tense lunch, Daisy kisses Gatsby and declares her love in front of Tom, who becomes alarmed and later reveals he has investigated Gatsby’s past, mocking his Oxford claim while Gatsby observes Daisy’s voice is 'full of money.' The group departs for New York in two cars—Gatsby driving his own yellow vehicle, Tom taking the blue coupé—and after a heated ride, they stop at Wilson’s garage in the Valley of Ashes, where Wilson, ill and desperate, reveals he plans to take Myrtle away, while Myrtle, mistaking Jordan for Tom’s wife, watches in jealous terror under the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg. At the Plaza Hotel, Tom confronts Gatsby with accusations of bootlegging via drugstores tied to Wolfsheim, and under pressure, Daisy admits she once loved Tom, shattering Gatsby’s idealized dream; Tom orders them to leave in Gatsby’s car, and they depart in silence. Later, Myrtle rushes out of the garage at dusk and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that flees, witnessed by Michaelis, who is unsure of its exact color; Tom and the narrator arrive at the scene to find Myrtle’s body covered on a worktable, Wilson hysterical, and Tom urgently denies involvement, claiming his blue coupé was not the car and later breaks down in tears, calling the driver a 'God damned coward.' That night, Nick waits outside the Buchanan house and finds Gatsby lurking in the shadows, who confesses Daisy was driving the fatal car but intends to take the blame and will wait outside her window all night; Nick sees through the pantry window that Tom and Daisy are quietly together in the kitchen, already reconciled, and Gatsby stands alone in the moonlight, vigilantly watching a house where his dream has already ended.</div>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">In the aftermath of Gatsby’s death, his mansion is overrun by police, photographers, and reporters, while the coroner’s dismissal of Wilson as a 'madman' and Catherine’s false testimony simplify the narrative into a tale of grief-driven violence. The narrator, feeling sole responsibility for Gatsby’s isolation, desperately tries to reach Daisy and Meyer Wolfshiem—calling Information, sending a letter via butler, and waiting for a wire—but receives no response, only a disconnected long-distance call from Chicago that mistakenly believed Gatsby was alive. On the third day, Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives by train from Minnesota, visibly shaken and bundled in a cheap ulster, displaying a worn photograph of the mansion and a childhood book with a handwritten self-improvement schedule, revealing Gatsby’s early ambition. Only Gatz and a Lutheran minister attend the funeral in a drizzling rain, as Klipspringer calls to request his tennis shoes and Wolfshiem refuses to come, citing fear of being 'mixed up' in death. At the cemetery, the man with owl-eyed glasses, drenched and wiping rain from his spectacles, arrives unexpectedly, murmurs 'Amen to that' after hearing 'Blessed are the dead that the rain falls on,' and later calls Gatsby 'the poor son-of-a-bitch.' The narrator, reflecting on his Midwestern roots during a Christmas train ride to Chicago, realizes he and his circle—Tom, Daisy, Jordan, Gatsby—are all Westerners fundamentally unfit for the East, which he now sees as a distorted, haunting landscape like an El Greco painting. He meets Jordan Baker, who reveals she is engaged to another man; he confronts her about her dishonesty and admits he is too old to lie to himself, and they part with unresolved emotion. On a late October afternoon, Nick encounters Tom Buchanan on Fifth Avenue, who admits he told Wilson that Gatsby owned the car that killed Myrtle, justifying it as self-defense and weeping over dog biscuits left in his flat. Nick, unable to forgive him, recognizes Tom and Daisy as careless people who destroy lives and retreat into wealth; he shakes Tom’s hand, feeling as if speaking to a child, as Tom enters a jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace or cuff buttons. On his final night in New York, the narrator walks to Gatsby’s overgrown mansion, erases an obscene word scrawled on the steps with his shoe, and wanders to the beach, reflecting on Gatsby’s futile dream of reclaiming the past through Daisy, culminating in the iconic image of boats beating against the current, borne back ceaselessly into the past.</div>\n                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Myrtle Wilson is a woman who seeks escape from her constrained life through relationships and displays of social aspiration. She exhibits a shift in demeanor when in the presence of Tom Buchanan, adopting an exaggerated sense of refinement, yet reveals vulnerability and insecurity in her personal history and relationships.</div>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6595f10d-dd12-40e9-9d88-7f5d63eb6af8","uuid":"e2c2db45-ee2e-4ea3-84dd-f9801f011926","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"As the summer unfolds, Mrs. Howard becomes increasingly drawn into the tangled web of relationships that define the era’s elite. She witnesses Tom’s affair with Myrtle Wilson, a vibrant but desperate woman whose life is crushed by the very system she seeks to escape. The revelation of Gatsby’s past—his real name, James Gatz, and his rise from pover