t in his flat. Nick, unable to forgive him, recognizes Tom and Daisy as careless people who destroy lives and retreat into wealth; he shakes Tom’s hand, feeling as if speaking to a child, as Tom enters a jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace or cuff buttons. On his final night in New York, the narrator walks to Gatsby’s overgrown mansion, erases an obscene word scrawled on the steps with his shoe, and wanders to the beach, reflecting on Gatsby’s futile dream of reclaiming the past through Daisy, culminating in the iconic image of boats beating against the current, borne back ceaselessly into the past.</div>\n                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Myrtle Wilson is a woman who seeks escape from her constrained life through relationships and displays of social aspiration. She exhibits a shift in demeanor when in the presence of Tom Buchanan, adopting an exaggerated sense of refinement, yet reveals vulnerability and insecurity in her personal history and relationships.</div>","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
